WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Chunking turns larger source documents into smaller units that can be embedded and retrieved.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:12.120
That process is useful, but it can separate text from the context that explains its permissions.

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:21.420
A paragraph may look harmless by itself while the source document, folder, project, or tenant indicates that only a limited audience should see it.

4
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:32.340
Index records should preserve links back to the source document, section, owner, tenant, classification, lifecycle status, and access control records.

5
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:39.400
If a chunk is copied into an index without those references, the retriever may not have the information it needs to filter results correctly.

6
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.980
Embeddings also deserve careful treatment.

7
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:50.000
They may not display the original text, but they can represent sensitive meaning and should be governed according to the source material.

8
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.480
Index design choices affect permission boundaries.

9
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.140
Some systems use per-tenant indexes.

10
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.760
Others use shared indexes with strict metadata filters.

11
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.540
Some combine keyword search with vector search and structured filters.

12
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.600
Any approach can work if the filtering rules are reliable, tested, and enforced consistently.

13
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.820
The risk increases when shared indexes are treated as a convenience layer without strong isolation logic.

14
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.900
Permission changes must reach the index.

15
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:25.560
If a document is deleted, reclassified, moved, or restricted, related chunks and embeddings need to be updated or excluded.

16
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.140
Re-indexing should account for changed ACLs and stale records.

17
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:37.840
Permission-aware chunk design reduces accidental overexposure by keeping security context attached to every searchable unit.
